WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.837
Coding Exercise 4 checks whether you understand the difference between matching and final filtering.

00:00:05.837 --> 00:00:12.627
The requirement says every patient must remain, while only Glucose lab rows should be allowed to match.

00:00:12.627 --> 00:00:16.727
That tells you the Glucose condition belongs in the matching logic.

00:00:16.727 --> 00:00:21.607
Complete the ON clause and do not move the test-name condition into WHERE.

00:00:21.607 --> 00:00:25.102
Before running the query, predict the shape of the result.

00:00:25.102 --> 00:00:30.422
Every patient should appear. Some patients will have a Glucose test ID and value.

00:00:30.422 --> 00:00:33.057
Others will have NULL lab fields.

00:00:33.057 --> 00:00:37.027
After Run and Check, inspect both types of rows.

00:00:37.027 --> 00:00:43.032
If only patients with Glucose tests remain, then the full patient population was not preserved.

00:00:43.032 --> 00:00:46.947
The purpose of this exercise is not just to pass the checker.

00:00:46.947 --> 00:00:48.662
It is to build a habit.

00:00:48.662 --> 00:00:55.302
Whenever you use an outer join, ask whether each condition belongs to the match or to the final result filter.

00:00:55.302 --> 00:00:58.462
That decision can change the meaning of the analysis.
